{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it shall come to pass, when all these things come upon you, etc.</b> One must know why it was necessary to say ״כָּל הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה״ (all these things), and it would not have sufficed to say ״כִּי יָבוֹאוּ עָלֶיךָ הַבְּרָכָה וְהַקְּלָלָה אֲשֶׁר נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ״ (when the blessing and the curse come upon you, which I have set before you)?" "<b>Or perhaps</b> it intends along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory, regarding blessings in tractate Berakhot (Berakhot 54a): a person is obligated to bless over the bad just as he blesses over the good, and the Gemara (Berakhot 60b) established this as receiving the bad with joy, in the same manner that one rejoices over the good." "<b>And this is</b> His saying ״וְהָיָה״ — which is an expression of joy (Genesis Rabbah 42:3) — ״כִּי יָבֹאוּ עָלֶיךָ כָּל הַדְּבָרִים״: receive them with joy, with the blessing and the curse placed on an equal footing together. For if it had said ״כִּי יָבֹאוּ עָלֶיךָ הַבְּרָכָה וְהַקְּלָלָה אֲשֶׁר נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ״, the verse would have been understood in this manner: ״וְהָיָה״ — which is the joy — ״כִּי יָבֹאוּ עָלֶיךָ הַבְּרָכָה״, while the word ״וְהַקְּלָלָה״ would be drawn along with the phrase ״אֲשֶׁר נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ״ that follows it, and I would not know that the expression ״וְהָיָה״ also extends over the curse." "<b>Furthermore,</b> I looked and saw how precious is the holy love with which our Elohim has loved us — do you not see that in mentioning the blessing, He said of it that it comes upon us: ״כִּי יָבֹאוּ עָלֶיךָ הַבְּרָכָה״, whereas in mentioning the curse He said ״וְהַקְּלָלָה אֲשֶׁר נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ״ — and not ״עָלֶיךָ״ (upon you). It is for this reason that He said ״אֲשֶׁר נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ״, and it would not have sufficed to say ״כָּל הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה״." "<b>Moreover,</b> if He had said ״כִּי יָבֹאוּ עָלֶיךָ הַבְּרָכָה וְהַקְּלָלָה אֲשֶׁר נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ״, I would have interpreted the phrase ״אֲשֶׁר נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ״ as applying to both of them. But through His saying ״כָּל הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה״, He revealed by this that the addition of ״אֲשֶׁר נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ״ comes to distinguish between the blessing and the curse, as I explained. And along this line, His saying ״כָּל הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה״ refers back to the blessing mentioned in proximity to it. And let it not seem wrong in your eyes that He said ״הַדְּבָרִים״ in the plural while הַבְּרָכָה (the blessing) is in the singular — for many good things are included in the blessing." "<b>The blessing and the curse.</b> That is, while the good is upon them — if they sinned and returned in repentance, they would be drawn close to HaShem; but even after they became abhorrent in the eyes of HaShem and He sent the fury of His anger against them and banished them from before Him, and on account of the trouble that surrounds them they return in repentance, as He said: ״וַהֲשֵׁבֹתָ וְגוֹ׳ בְּכָל הַגּוֹיִם״ — even so, HaShem will restore their fortunes." "<b>And there is</b> reason to note why it was necessary to say הַבְּרָכָה, since the matter that surrounds the repentance is the fear of the curse — it should have said only ״כִּי יָבֹא עָלֶיךָ הַקְּלָלָה״. And it appears that it comes to note a matter through which one will take to heart to return in repentance, namely that one will understand — when He deals well with one's deeds before HaShem blessed be He, the blessing that surrounds one, and when one departs from the path of life, the curse that comes upon one — and from this one will understand to take to heart, for aside from the test of the blessing that comes through doing His will blessed be He, one will not find the cause of the curse, which is on account of having abandoned HaShem." "<b>וַהֲשֵׁבֹתָ אֶל לְבָבֶךָ.</b> That is, because the wicked — their heart leads astray, therefore it says ״וַהֲשֵׁבֹתָ אֶל לְבָבֶךָ״ — both of those that go astray on a path not good. And His saying ״בְּכָל הַגּוֹיִם״ etc. — meaning, through the force of the argument of one's being exiled and scattered among all the nations, one will say to one's heart: ״See what the deeds of the evildoers caused,״ and one will know that it is evil and bitter to have abandoned HaShem. And this is His saying ״וַהֲשֵׁבֹתָ אֶל לְבָבֶךָ״ by the argument of ״כָּל הַגּוֹיִם אֲשֶׁר הִדִּיחֲךָ ה׳ שָׁמָּה״ — as if it said: as the place where HaShem has banished you among all the nations." "<b>And the reason</b> that Scripture spoke in this manner is that the seducer might find an argument to refute the proof of the exiles, saying: for behold, how many nations experience such a thing, that they are exiled from their land. Therefore it says ״בְּכָל הַגּוֹיִם״ — for there is no exile like the exile of Israel, who were scattered to the four winds of the world. And this is His saying ״בְּכָל הַגּוֹיִם אֲשֶׁר הִדִּיחֲךָ ה׳ שָׁמָּה״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:38.730325646Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}